1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:04,392
How do thieves steal card numbers without breaking into any database?

2
00:00:04,442 --> 00:00:07,272
They add one line of script to a payment page.

3
00:00:07,322 --> 00:00:12,144
It might arrive through a hacked analytics tool, or a changed file on a shared server.

4
00:00:12,194 --> 00:00:17,280
That one line copies every card number as the buyer types it, and sends it away.

5
00:00:17,330 --> 00:00:18,816
The page still works.

6
00:00:18,866 --> 00:00:20,688
The buyer still pays.

7
00:00:20,738 --> 00:00:22,656
Nobody notices for weeks.

8
00:00:22,706 --> 00:00:24,528
This is called web skimming.

9
00:00:24,578 --> 00:00:27,048
The card zone's answer is the card frame.

10
00:00:27,098 --> 00:00:31,320
The frame is the card zone's own small page, on its own address.

11
00:00:31,370 --> 00:00:34,176
It sits inside the bank's payment page like a window.

12
00:00:34,226 --> 00:00:37,296
Think of it as the bank's card reader on the shop's counter.

13
00:00:37,346 --> 00:00:39,768
A real card reader has tamper seals.

14
00:00:39,818 --> 00:00:41,640
The frame has seals too.

15
00:00:41,690 --> 00:00:45,864
Seal one: only the page its session names may hold the frame.

16
00:00:45,914 --> 00:00:50,688
Each time a buyer pays, the card zone opens a session for one page.

17
00:00:50,738 --> 00:00:54,792
A look-alike site that tries to hold the frame is refused by the browser.

18
00:00:54,842 --> 00:00:57,840
Seal two: a Content Security Policy.

19
00:00:57,890 --> 00:01:00,456
That is a rule the frame gives the browser.

20
00:01:00,506 --> 00:01:05,592
It says: run nothing but my own two files, and talk only to my own address.

21
00:01:05,642 --> 00:01:08,712
Any other script is refused before it runs.

22
00:01:08,762 --> 00:01:11,856
Seal three: Subresource Integrity.

23
00:01:11,906 --> 00:01:15,168
The frame names each of its two files by a fingerprint.

24
00:01:15,218 --> 00:01:21,168
If even one character of a file changes, the fingerprint no longer matches, and the browser will not run it.

25
00:01:21,218 --> 00:01:27,576
When the buyer finishes, the frame hands back only two things: a token and the masked number.

26
00:01:27,626 --> 00:01:31,248
It hands them only to the one page its session names.

27
00:01:31,298 --> 00:01:34,944
The card number itself never leaves the frame for the page.

28
00:01:34,994 --> 00:01:38,400
When the browser blocks something, it reports it to the card zone.

29
00:01:38,450 --> 00:01:41,328
So a tampering attempt leaves a trace.

30
00:01:41,378 --> 00:01:46,896
And the shop's page around the frame is watched too, because a skimmer there could still trick the buyer.

31
00:01:46,946 --> 00:01:48,768
Now watch the product.

32
00:01:48,818 --> 00:01:50,760
The buyer opens the payment page.

33
00:01:50,810 --> 00:01:55,704
The frame appears inside it, with two fields: card number and expiry date.

34
00:01:55,754 --> 00:01:59,064
As the buyer types, the number falls into groups of four.

35
00:01:59,114 --> 00:02:02,328
The expiry date gets its slash by itself.

36
00:02:02,378 --> 00:02:07,176
The buyer presses "Send code", types the code from their phone, and presses "Confirm".

37
00:02:07,226 --> 00:02:10,728
The frame says "Card accepted" and clears the number field.

38
00:02:10,778 --> 00:02:13,320
The page shows only the masked line.

39
00:02:13,370 --> 00:02:15,192
Now try to break it.

40
00:02:15,242 --> 00:02:18,960
Open the frame's address on its own, outside the payment page.

41
00:02:19,010 --> 00:02:23,808
It shows one sentence: this page opens only inside the payment page.

42
00:02:23,858 --> 00:02:25,680
No form appears.

43
00:02:25,730 --> 00:02:28,272
Put the frame inside a look-alike site.

44
00:02:28,322 --> 00:02:31,584
The browser shows an empty, refused box instead.

45
00:02:31,634 --> 00:02:33,456
Now act as a skimmer.

46
00:02:33,506 --> 00:02:36,960
Inside the frame, try to load a script from another address.

47
00:02:37,010 --> 00:02:42,192
The browser's console shows a red line: the script is refused under the frame's policy.

48
00:02:42,242 --> 00:02:44,064
Nothing loads.

49
00:02:44,114 --> 00:02:47,712
On the bank's side, that refusal arrives as a report.

50
00:02:47,762 --> 00:02:52,800
The team sees it in the card zone's logs, with the rule that was broken and the page it came from.

51
00:02:52,850 --> 00:02:54,672
No card data is in it.

52
00:02:54,722 --> 00:02:56,544
What does this protect?

53
00:02:56,594 --> 00:03:03,048
It protects the one moment a card number exists in plain view: in the buyer's browser, as it is typed.

54
00:03:03,098 --> 00:03:08,520
A database can be locked and sealed, and a skimmer can still copy numbers at the keyboard.

55
00:03:08,570 --> 00:03:11,160
The seals protect the buyer from a stranger's script.

56
00:03:11,210 --> 00:03:13,392
They protect the bank from a stolen form.

57
00:03:13,442 --> 00:03:17,208
And they protect the shop, whose page never touches the number.

58
00:03:17,258 --> 00:03:19,080
Back to the card reader.

59
00:03:19,130 --> 00:03:21,336
It runs only the bank's software.

60
00:03:21,386 --> 00:03:23,208
It works only on one counter.

61
00:03:23,258 --> 00:03:25,080
And a broken seal shows.

62
00:03:25,130 --> 00:03:27,696
Next: the two doors into the locked room.
